{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 5, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**A Whirlwind Trip to Japan**\n\nJapan dazzled me with its blend of ancient traditions and cutting-edge modernity. I arrived in Tokyo, where neon-lit streets clashed with serene shrines. A visit to Senso-ji Temple in Asakusa transported me to another era, its giant red lantern a welcoming sight. Nearby, the lively Nakamise shopping street offered tasty street food—fresh mochi and melon pan—while modern skyscrapers loomed overhead.\n\nKyoto stole my heart with its timeless beauty. Fushimi Inari’s endless torii gates led me up the mountain, each one inscribed with donors' names. Arashiyama’s bamboo forest whispered in the wind, while a traditional tea ceremony in Gion revealed the grace of matcha preparation.\n\nOsaka’s Dotonbori pulsed with energy. Takoyaki in hand, I marveled at the giant crab sign and neon billboards reflecting in the canal. The friendly locals, or *osaka-jan*, made me feel right at home.\n\nA day trip to Hiroshima was humbling. The Peace Memorial Park and Museum reminded me of history’s weight, but the city’s resilience shone through. A short ferry ride to Miyajima Island introduced me to the iconic floating torii gate of Itsukushima Shrine.\n\nJapan’s efficiency astounded me—spotless streets, punctual trains, and vending machines on every corner. Yet, its warmth lingered most: a stranger bowing in thanks, a shopkeeper’s patient smile despite my broken Japanese.\n\nFrom bullet trains whisking me across landscapes to quiet onsens tucked in mountains, Japan was a paradox—fast yet peaceful, crowded yet intimate. I left with memories etched deeper than any kanji, already dreaming of my return.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.08057959377765656}, {"token": "A", "logprob": -0.08920913934707642}, {"token": " Wh", "logprob": -0.540644645690918}, {"token": "irl", "logprob": -9.65590606938349e-06}, {"token": "wind", "logpr